  {
   "date": "2026-11-09",
   "text": [
    "As of 9 November the date named in the arc's question has passed, and the binding decisions of the week were taken locally rather than federally. Voters in 10 states decided 41 ballot measures on data centres on 3 November, and CNBC reported that they \"backed restrictions or rejected pro-development measures in all but one\". No source read this week reports a floor vote on a federal frontier-AI bill before the election. Reuters reported that the incoming Democratic Congress plans bills on AI testing, liability and data-centre electricity costs; none has been introduced. The Justice Department announced a Super Intelligence Task Force to prosecute AI misuse, folding in its existing AI Litigation Section — a prosecutorial body rather than a regulator.",
    "The companies moved before the legislators did. OpenAI, which this briefing reported on 2 November was planning a post-midterms push for mandatory AI \"speed limits\", published a policy blueprint and a set of commitments on 5 November: paying the full cost of powering the data centres it owns and operates, soliciting community input, publishing construction plans, and no longer using non-disclosure agreements with public officials. Its chief global affairs officer said the blueprint lays out \"the best lessons that we have learned\" and encouraged policymakers to enact similar commitments as laws. Nothing in the week binds any lab: the blueprint is voluntary and scoped to campuses OpenAI owns and operates, and Reuters notes partners such as Oracle build and own much of its infrastructure."
   ],
   "changed": "The deadline in the question arrived. The midterm elections were held on 3 November and no source read this week reports a federal frontier-AI measure reaching a floor vote beforehand, which leaves the local and state route the arc has been tracking as the operative one: voters in 10 states decided 41 data-centre measures and backed restrictions in all but one. What is new is a lab writing the rules in advance of the legislators. OpenAI published a policy blueprint and voluntary commitments two days after the vote, including dropping non-disclosure agreements with public officials, and asked policymakers to turn them into law. The Justice Department created a Super Intelligence Task Force for prosecutions, and Reuters reports bills on AI testing and liability planned for the new Congress but not yet introduced."
  }
 ],
 "figures": [

